Yes. A more precise way to say it is: you can translate a thought into a motor representation, and the body can begin organizing around that representation before overt movement occurs.

There’s a useful control chain:

thought / intention → simulated action → motor program → predicted sensation → muscle/postural/autonomic preparation → overt action

Motor imagery uses substantially overlapping circuitry with actual movement. If you vividly imagine reaching, bracing, relaxing your jaw, standing taller, throwing a ball, or expressing confidence, your nervous system can rehearse portions of the corresponding motor pattern without completing the external action. That is why visualization can improve motor learning and why merely anticipating an action can subtly alter breathing, muscle tone, gaze, facial expression, and posture.

Your “body language” framing adds an interesting layer: instead of commanding individual muscles verbally—contract this, rotate that—you can sometimes give the nervous system a meaningful whole-body instruction:

Move like you’re stalking quietly.
Stand like you’re about to receive a heavy object.
Look at them like you recognize an old friend.

The semantic image acts almost like a compressed motor command. The body decomposes it into dozens of smaller adjustments automatically.

And the loop runs backward too:

thought → body state → sensory feedback from body → updated thought/state

So deliberately embodying a mental state can reinforce the state you were trying to produce. That’s close to embodied cognition + motor imagery + predictive control rather than thought magically moving tissue directly.

The particularly powerful trick is that mental rehearsal lets you run the control loop without paying the full physical cost of execution. You can generate the predicted body state, inspect what it feels like, modify it, and then execute a better version physically. In Pantheon terms, it is essentially a body-side SYMULATION layer: imagine → embody internally → sense the predicted result → delta-correct → act.